WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.248
Here is a question that a lot of people are too shy to ask.

2
00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:10.992
To log in to a server with SSH keys, you hand out your public key.

3
00:00:10.992 --> 00:00:13.392
And public means anyone may see it.

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.707
So what stops a stranger from copying it, and logging in as you?

5
00:00:17.707 --> 00:00:19.311
Hold on to that question.

6
00:00:19.311 --> 00:00:21.172
We will answer it on the way.

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.523
Let's start with what you already know.

8
00:00:24.523 --> 00:00:29.309
SSH gives you a shell on a far away machine, over an encrypted connection.

9
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:40.484
Running ssh ubuntu@192.168.64.5 logs you in as ubuntu, on the machine at that address.

10
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:46.011
With a password, you type the secret, and the secret itself travels to the server.

11
00:00:46.011 --> 00:00:47.177
Every single time.

12
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.307
And people guess passwords, and reuse them.

13
00:00:50.307 --> 00:00:54.432
Use the same one in five places, and one leak opens all five.

14
00:00:54.633 --> 00:01:00.933
A password is like a house key that you hand to the front door every morning, and trust the door to give it back.

15
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:03.120
So the real problem is simple.

16
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.968
To be useful, a password has to leave home.

17
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.647
Wouldn't it be nice if you could prove who you are, and your secret never left your laptop at all?

18
00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:15.150
Let's step into our office building for a moment.

19
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.991
You need to work in another building, across town.

20
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.306
Here is the trick.

21
00:01:19.306 --> 00:01:22.933
You own a padlock that you are happy to hand out to anyone.

22
00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:26.007
And you own one key, the only thing that opens it.

23
00:01:26.233 --> 00:01:29.740
You give the other building's guard a copy of your padlock.

24
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:33.841
He hangs it on his board, next to the padlocks of everyone he trusts.

25
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:36.057
The key never leaves your desk.

26
00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:37.905
Now let's map it.

27
00:01:37.905 --> 00:01:39.944
The key is your private key.

28
00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:42.285
The padlock is your public key.

29
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.990
And the guard's board is a file on the server, called authorized_keys.

30
00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:48.529
One public key per line.

31
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:53.379
You make the pair with one command, ssh-keygen.

32
00:01:53.379 --> 00:02:01.781
It writes two files, id_ed25519, the private key, and id_ed25519.pub, the public one.

33
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.748
So, back to our question.

34
00:02:03.748 --> 00:02:07.944
A stranger who copies your public key gets, well, a padlock.

35
00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:10.097
A padlock can't open anything.

36
00:02:10.097 --> 00:02:12.671
It can only check the key that opens it.

37
00:02:12.671 --> 00:02:14.730
A public key works the same way.

38
00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.175
It can check, but it can never log in.

39
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.697
But how does the guard know that you hold the key?

40
00:02:20.697 --> 00:02:24.000
The obvious answer: walk the key over and show it to him.

41
00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:24.566
Well.

42
00:02:24.566 --> 00:02:29.422
Now your secret has left home, which is the one thing we wanted to avoid.

43
00:02:29.422 --> 00:02:32.083
That's just a password with extra steps.

44
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.974
So here is where the padlock story stops.

45
00:02:34.974 --> 00:02:38.756
On the real wire, nothing gets locked, and no key travels.

46
00:02:38.967 --> 00:02:44.883
When you connect, there is a small piece of data that belongs to this one connection, and to no other.

47
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.647
Your laptop has three things to work with.

48
00:02:47.647 --> 00:02:50.498
The private key, the public key, and that data.

49
00:02:50.498 --> 00:02:53.227
Which one crosses the wire to prove it's you?

50
00:02:53.433 --> 00:02:54.720
Pause and guess.

51
00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:57.819
None of them.

52
00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:01.991
Your ssh takes the data, and signs it with the private key.

53
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.532
Out comes something new, a signature.

54
00:03:04.733 --> 00:03:07.215
And only the signature crosses the wire.

55
00:03:07.833 --> 00:03:11.595
The server checks it with your public key, from authorized_keys.

56
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:12.674
It fits.

57
00:03:12.674 --> 00:03:14.203
So you are in.

58
00:03:14.433 --> 00:03:17.640
And the private key itself never left your laptop.

59
00:03:18.267 --> 00:03:22.059
What if someone records that signature, and tries it tomorrow?

60
00:03:22.059 --> 00:03:23.037
Pause and guess.

61
00:03:25.033 --> 00:03:29.906
Tomorrow's connection has different data, so yesterday's signature fits nothing.

62
00:03:29.906 --> 00:03:32.769
It's about as useful as yesterday's bus ticket.

63
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.591
A signature proves it's you once, for one connection only.

64
00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.049
Trust also runs the other way.

65
00:03:39.049 --> 00:03:43.485
What if the building you walk into is a fake, set up to look like yours?

66
00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.845
Every server has a fingerprint, a short code that identifies it.

67
00:03:48.067 --> 00:03:55.284
The first time you connect, ssh shows you that fingerprint, and asks if you are sure you want to continue connecting.

68
00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.859
Say yes, and ssh writes it down in a file on your laptop, called known_hosts.

69
00:04:00.859 --> 00:04:03.087
Your notebook of familiar faces.

70
00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:09.126
Next time, ssh compares the server's fingerprint with the one in the notebook.

71
00:04:09.126 --> 00:04:11.073
Same face, so it carries on.

72
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:19.216
If that fingerprint ever changes, ssh refuses to connect, because someone may be pretending to be your server.

73
00:04:19.433 --> 00:04:26.115
ssh is the friend who says, that's not our usual doorman, and won't go in, however nice the uniform.

74
00:04:26.115 --> 00:04:29.857
So a refusal is a warning to read, not a hurdle to jump.

75
00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:32.525
Three habits keep this safe.

76
00:04:32.525 --> 00:04:32.804
One.

77
00:04:32.804 --> 00:04:35.672
Never copy the private key anywhere else.

78
00:04:35.672 --> 00:04:37.420
It works like a password.

79
00:04:37.633 --> 00:04:37.929
Two.

80
00:04:37.929 --> 00:04:40.734
Set a passphrase when ssh-keygen asks.

81
00:04:40.734 --> 00:04:46.123
It encrypts the private key file, so a stolen copy is useless without it.

82
00:04:46.333 --> 00:04:46.870
Three.

83
00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:54.560
ls -l shows the private key with mode 600, for you alone, and the public key with 644.

84
00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:57.775
So here is the moral of the story.

85
00:04:57.775 --> 00:05:00.899
The secret stays home, and only proof travels.

86
00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.010
The padlock goes everywhere.

87
00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:04.442
The key goes nowhere.

88
00:05:04.442 --> 00:05:06.693
Every login is a fresh signature.

89
00:05:06.693 --> 00:05:09.831
And known_hosts remembers the faces you trust.

90
00:05:10.633 --> 00:05:12.413
A quick check before you go.

91
00:05:12.413 --> 00:05:15.273
A new teammate needs to log in to the server.

92
00:05:15.273 --> 00:05:18.070
What do they send you, and where does it go?

93
00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:19.087
Pause and guess.

94
00:05:21.267 --> 00:05:24.868
Their public key, as one more line in authorized_keys.

95
00:05:24.868 --> 00:05:27.936
Their private key stays home, on their laptop.
